{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794535096088,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2a07e880025ng37nA6lgmSP1","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a072cd001xeM4RJxkbfrcqm","type":"tool","id":"call_ed51420e4100409c90621814","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git -c core.fsmonitor=false show 0bfe4d6e92 -- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json"},"output":"commit 0bfe4d6e929c7e04820d4619787cdffca00108fb\nAuthor: Michel Kerkmeester <82775228+MichelKerkmeester@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Fri Nov 6 10:31:03 2026 +0100\n\n    feat(system-skilled-doctor): add /doctor:overlay repin with a staged preview and rollback\n    \n    Moving a vendored Skilled copy to a newer upstream commit was a seven-step\n    hand runbook with no way back, because the vendored tree is untracked.\n    \n    repin now does it in one guarded run. It computes one set of touched\n    paths from the two commits, refuses while a file no overlay owns differs\n    from the pinned commit, and backs up every path it will touch before its\n    first write. Then it copies, sets the pin and applies the overlays twice.\n    --stage runs the same steps on a copy as the plan, and --rollback restores\n    a run's backup byte for byte.\n    \n    After a moved pin, a destination that still holds its pinned upstream\n    bytes now reads overlay-edited, so apply can write over it. Before, every\n    re-pin blocked on the runtime folders it had just copied.\n    \n    Spec: system-skilled-doctor/001-skill-and-overlay-build/003-repin\n    Commit-Id: 0013055\n\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json\nindex ea28733ee4..7bc5f183ca 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json\n@@ -322,6 +322,12 @@\n       \"order\": 38,\n       \"id\": \"command-doctor-overlay-apply\",\n       \"description\": \"\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"inventoryId\": \"command-doctor-overlay-repin\",\n+      \"order\": 39,\n+      \"id\": \"command-doctor-overlay-repin\",\n+      \"description\": \"\"\n     }\n   ],\n   \"python\": [\n@@ -687,6 +693,12 @@\n       \"order\": 49,\n       \"id\": \"command-doctor-overlay-apply\",\n       \"description\": \"\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"inventoryId\": \"command-doctor-overlay-repin\",\n+      \"order\": 50,\n+      \"id\": \"command-doctor-overlay-repin\",\n+      \"description\": \"\"\n     }\n   ],\n   \"pythonOwnerNormalization\": {\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"commit 0bfe4d6e929c7e04820d4619787cdffca00108fb\nAuthor: Michel Kerkmeester <82775228+MichelKerkmeester@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Fri Nov 6 10:31:03 2026 +0100\n\n    feat(system-skilled-doctor): add /doctor:overlay repin with a staged preview and rollback\n    \n    Moving a vendored Skilled copy to a newer upstream commit was a seven-step\n    hand runbook with no way back, because the vendored tree is untracked.\n    \n    repin now does it in one guarded run. It computes one set of touched\n    paths from the two commits, refuses while a file no overlay owns differs\n    from the pinned commit, and backs up every path it will touch before its\n    first write. Then it copies, sets the pin and applies the overlays twice.\n    --stage runs the same steps on a copy as the plan, and --rollback restores\n    a run's backup byte for byte.\n    \n    After a moved pin, a destination that still holds its pinned upstream\n    bytes now reads overlay-edited, so apply can write over it. Before, every\n    re-pin blocked on the runtime folders it had just copied.\n    \n    Spec: system-skilled-doctor/001-skill-and-overlay-build/003-repin\n    Commit-Id: 0013055\n\ndiff --git a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json\nindex ea28733ee4..7bc5f183ca 100644\n--- a/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json\n+++ b/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json\n@@ -322,6 +322,12 @@\n       \"order\": 38,\n       \"id\": \"command-doctor-overlay-apply\",\n       \"description\": \"\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"inventoryId\": \"command-doctor-overlay-repin\",\n+      \"order\": 39,\n+      \"id\": \"command-doctor-overlay-repin\",\n+      \"description\": \"\"\n     }\n   ],\n   \"python\": [\n@@ -687,6 +693,12 @@\n       \"order\": 49,\n       \"id\": \"command-doctor-overlay-apply\",\n       \"description\": \"\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"inventoryId\": \"command-doctor-overlay-repin\",\n+      \"order\": 50,\n+      \"id\": \"command-doctor-overlay-repin\",\n+      \"description\": \"\"\n     }\n   ],\n   \"pythonOwnerNormalization\": {\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && git -c core.fsmonitor=false show 0bfe4d6e92 -- .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/scoring-compatibility.json\"}"},"time":{"start":1794535095944,"end":1794535096088}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794535098428,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"id":"prt_1d2a0883c001wARWkZ45Qhmlc3","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a072cd001xeM4RJxkbfrcqm","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"6dc47500d17d0ec2ff679b9a6ea456bfcede6ecc","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":1394,"output":104,"reasoning":68,"cache":{"read":60544,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794535100815,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"id":"prt_1d2a0918f001TQKeziq8nd8cQj","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a08841001F3Dk0d13i56PXK","type":"step-start","snapshot":"f00ec918b65ae723d41440c77bf5374d4bb923e8"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794535107370,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2a0a7e8002XXA4YR03ozHNGR","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a08841001F3Dk0d13i56PXK","type":"tool","id":"call_635a998f80a54e0ca6aa56e4","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794535106536,"end":1794535107370}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794535107411,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2a0a94f001ToUt2LcaI0Q2JT","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a08841001F3Dk0d13i56PXK","type":"tool","id":"call_949df36f26fe4cc7bd08c656","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md, lines 1-583\n1: ---\n2: title: sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n3: description: First-class surface router document at the sk-code hub root (ROUTER.md). hub-router.json selects the workflow mode; this doc maps a request's code intent to the exact packet-local leaf resources that mode should load, emitting canonical (workflowMode, leafResourceId) pairs. Relocated from shared/references/smart-routing.md (v3.5.0.8) as the ROUTER.md consolidation pattern, with the approved sk-code shared-control delta (router self-reference removed, ten hub-shared paths normalized to shared/..., mapped shared controls declared).\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code smart routing\"\n6:   - \"surface resource maps\"\n7:   - \"intent classification routing\"\n8:   - \"load tier resource loading\"\n9:   - \"unknown surface fallback\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 2.3.19.0\n13: router_state: active\n14: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n15: ---\n16: # sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n17: \n18: Leaf-set authority for sk-code. The routing authority is named once in `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING: `hub-router.json` is the mode vocabulary, and this document is the leaf-set router. Consolidates intent classification + resource loading into the single Barter-style smart-routing entry point. Detection logic lives in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md); phase lifecycle lives in [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: ### Purpose\n25: \n26: The smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n27: \n28: ### When to Use\n29: \n30: - After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n31: - When classifying task wording into the dominant work intent\n32: - When mapping intent to concrete reference paths\n33: - When picking verification commands for the surface\n34: \n35: ### Core Principle\n36: \n37: Routing is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n38: \n39: ### Key Sources\n40: \n41: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n42: - [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and phase-specific resource expectations\n43: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n48: \n49: Intent classification scores task text against weighted keyword signals to pick the dominant work type after surface detection. The vocabulary and weights are authored once, in the `INTENT_SIGNALS` block of §8 MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER; read them there. This section is the human-facing summary of how that block is scored, never a second copy of its keywords.\n50: \n51: ### Scoring algorithm\n52: \n53: 1. Sum weighted keyword hits from the request, target files, and known task context.\n54: 2. Boost explicit phase signals (verification, debugging, code quality) by +5 when the phase is unambiguous.\n55: 3. Select the top intent.\n56: 4. Select a second intent when the score delta to the next intent is small (`AMBIGUITY_DELTA = 1`).\n57: 5. For SKILLED surface, run language sub-detection after intent selection.\n58: \n59: ### Doc-only edit anti-signals\n60: \n61: Doc-only prose changes subtract from `sk-code` intent scoring and add to `sk-doc` scoring, even when the target file is under `.skilled/skills/`.\n62: \n63: | Prompt signal | Effect |\n64: | --- | --- |\n65: | `update headline`, `rewrite headline`, `headline section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n66: | `clarify description`, `clarify the X model`, `documentation section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n67: | `add a one-line summary`, `add summary at the top` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n68: | `improve readme`, `reorganize readme`, `rewrite section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n69: | `SKILL.md` or `README.md` plus prose-only verbs | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n70: \n71: Do not apply this anti-signal when the same request asks to modify executable code, parser logic, tests, shell behavior, TypeScript/Python entrypoints, JSON schema behavior, or routing algorithms. In that case, keep SKILLED surface detection and load `sk-code`.\n72: \n73: Multi-symptom prompts like `debug the broken jsonc descriptor` should load both DEBUGGING and CONFIG. Prompts like `update TypeScript advisor fixture` should load SKILLED plus TYPESCRIPT and CODE_QUALITY.\n74: \n75: ### Surface-specific intent notes\n76: \n77: - **SKILLED** intent scoring favors language standards, hooks, config, scripts, advisor/tests, metadata, and alignment verification.\n78: - Unsupported surfaces stay UNKNOWN even if intent scoring is strong.\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 3. LOAD TIERS\n83: \n84: | Tier | When | Resources |\n85: | --- | --- | --- |\n86: | ALWAYS | Every invocation | The §8 `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble only: `shared/references/stack-detection.md`, `shared/references/phase-detection.md` and `shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`. The style guide `sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md` loads with CODE_QUALITY, and the shared doctrine loads with its intent: `workflow-debug.md` with DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` with VERIFICATION |\n87: | SURFACE | After SKILLED detection | Surface-specific resources (`sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`) |\n88: | INTENT | After intent classification | Implementation, debugging, verification, testing, etc. matching the top-1 intent (and top-2 when ambiguous) |\n89: | LANGUAGE | SKILLED only | Split JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and Config standards from the matching `sk-code-skilled/references/<lang>/` resources |\n90: | ON_DEMAND | Explicit deep-dive keywords | Extended checklists and niche references (e.g. `sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md` for boundary templates) |\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 4. SKILLED MAP\n95: \n96: SKILLED loads from `sk-code-skilled/references/` and `sk-code-skilled/assets/`.\n97: \n98: ### Implementation shared tier\n99: \n100: IMPLEMENTATION loads the shared organization and naming baseline:\n101: \n102: - `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`\n103: - `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`\n104: \n105: ### Intent overlay\n106: \n107: | Intent | Resources |\n108: | --- | --- |\n109: | CODE_QUALITY | SKILLED only: `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` plus the language-matching checklist, both when SKILLED is a resolved surface. A foreign surface loads the floor in `code-quality-standards.md` and its own declared rows |\n110: | VERIFICATION | `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` |\n111: | HOOKS | `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/hooks.md` |\n112: | CONFIG | `sk-code-skilled/references/config/*` |\n113: | LANGUAGE_STANDARDS | Detected language folder's `quick-reference.md`, `style-guide.md`, `quality-standards.md` |\n114: \n115: ### Language overlay\n116: \n117: | Language | Resources |\n118: | --- | --- |\n119: | JAVASCRIPT | `sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n120: | TYPESCRIPT | `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n121: | PYTHON | `sk-code-skilled/references/python/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n122: | SHELL | `sk-code-skilled/references/shell/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n123: | RUST | `sk-code-skilled/references/rust/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}/` (each split into topic parts), `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/` |\n124: | CONFIG | `sk-code-skilled/references/config/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n125: \n126: ### Authoring checklist gating\n127: \n128: When SKILLED intent is `authoring-new-X`, additionally load the matching authoring checklist:\n129: \n130: | Authoring target | Checklist |\n131: | --- | --- |\n132: | New skill | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n133: | New agent | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n134: | New command | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n135: | New MCP server | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md` |\n136: | Spec folder write | Now in system-spec-kit: `system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` + `system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` |\n137: \n138: ### CI Workflow Map\n139: \n140: When the request names a GitHub Actions workflow, the hub bundles `sk-code-ci` from the request vocabulary and the `CI_WORKFLOW` intent loads its four rule references:\n141: \n142: | Resource | Carries |\n143: | --- | --- |\n144: | `sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` | The rule index: path parity, SHA pins, README row, `set -euo pipefail`, fork and secret safety, matrix and `fail-fast` |\n145: | `sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md` | The twin rule, the rebuild-commit skip rule, push-only scope and the intentional exceptions table |\n146: | `sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md` | Timeouts, permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch `if:` shape |\n147: | `sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md` | The repository CI profile and its keys, the workflows protected by function and the rules that follow |\n148: \n149: The map is the packet's own `RESOURCE_MAP` re-prefixed with `sk-code-ci/`, so the two stay equal.\n150: \n151: ### Frontend Map\n152: \n153: When a request asks about web frontend practice, or a Figma-to-code check, the hub bundles `sk-code-frontend` from request vocabulary alone:\n154: \n155: | Resource | Carries |\n156: | --- | --- |\n157: | `sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md` | The cross-stack core, token precedence, Figma boundary, and Frontend overrides contract |\n158: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md` | The web stack layer for markup, forms, layout, responsive behavior, media, and CSS architecture |\n159: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-elements-and-attributes.md` | Form autocomplete, dialog, and popover behavior |\n160: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md` | Cascade layers and specificity conflicts |\n161: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-layout-and-sizing.md` | Container queries and stacking contexts |\n162: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md` | Accessibility patterns, visible focus, and live regions |\n163: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-performance-and-loading.md` | Web font loading and largest contentful paint |\n164: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-internationalization.md` | Right-to-left layout and bidirectional text |\n165: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md` | Visual value decisions: spacing, type, weight, colour, shadow, radius, opacity, palettes and token sets |\n166: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md` | Visual hierarchy and review: build procedure, symptom-to-fix tables, UX laws and the review pass |\n167: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-motion-and-interaction.md` | Motion and interaction: animation, input, touch, focus, performance and feedback rules |\n168: | `sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md` | The five-pass check for comparing a built screen with its Figma frame |\n169: \n170: The map is the packet's own `RESOURCE_MAP` for the web, visual and Figma-to-code intents, re-prefixed with `sk-code-frontend/`, so the two stay equal there.\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## 5. VERIFICATION COMMANDS\n175: \n176: | Surface | Required verification evidence |\n177: | --- | --- |\n178: | SKILLED | `python3 .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py --root <changed-scope>`; plus targeted language/project tests such as vitest, pytest, shellcheck, JSON validation, or spec validation for changed spec folders |\n179: | UNKNOWN | User-selected verification command set before completion claim |\n180: \n181: ---\n182: \n183: ## 6. UNKNOWN FALLBACK\n184: \n185: If no supported surface matches, ask:\n186: \n187: 1. Is this `.skilled/` system code, or an unsupported stack?\n188: 2. Which files or directories are changing?\n189: 3. Which verification command proves the claim?\n190: 4. Should a new `sk-code` route be planned before implementation?\n191: \n192: Canonical sk-code owns the SKILLED surface, the `sk-code-ci` GitHub Actions surface and the stack-agnostic `sk-code-frontend` surface for web practice. Go, Next.js server code and React Native surfaces live in a project fork.\n193: \n194: ### UNKNOWN fallback checklist\n195: \n196: Returned when intent confidence is low (`max(intent_scores) < 0.5`) OR when the user explicitly asks for stack-agnostic guidance:\n197: \n198: - Confirm the detected surface (a declared specific surface, the SKILLED fallback or UNKNOWN)\n199: - Confirm the task intent (implementation / debugging / verification / etc.)\n200: - Provide one concrete input, error, or expected output\n201: - Confirm the verification command set before completion\n202: \n203: ---\n204: \n205: ## 7. LOADING DISCIPLINE (DO / DON'T)\n206: \n207: ### DO\n208: \n209: - Load only references needed for current intent + surface\n210: - Use exact canonical file paths in playbook result YAML (never directory placeholders)\n211: - Cite surface + top-1 intent + loaded resource paths + verification command in your final answer\n212: \n213: ### DON'T\n214: \n215: - Load all of `sk-code-skilled/` indiscriminately (\"just in case\")\n216: - Load Go, Next.js server code or React Native resources from canonical sk-code; those live in a project fork\n217: - Drop directory placeholders in playbook result YAML\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 8. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n222: \n223: This block is the authored source for the intent vocabulary and weights. §2 explains how it is scored and does not repeat the keywords; §4 renders the same rows as prose. When an intent row changes here, update the matching prose row in §4.\n224: \n225: This projection is intentionally lossy in two documented ways the flat dictionary cannot express, both enforced by the prose contract and the surface-detection pseudocode in this file's §11:\n226: \n227: - **No phase boosts.** The `+5` unambiguous-phase boost from the §2 scoring algorithm is not represented; every keyword carries unit weight here.\n228: - **No doc-only anti-signals.** The `sk-code -2 / sk-doc +3` anti-signals (§2) that route prose-only edits away from this skill are a pre-filter the flat model does not carry.\n229: \n230: ```python\n231: # Always-loaded routing preamble: every code task detects stack, detects phase,\n232: # consults the router, and applies the universal quality baseline before any\n233: # surface-specific resource. These are loaded on every route.\n234: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n235:     \"shared/references/stack-detection.md\",\n236:     \"shared/references/phase-detection.md\",\n237:     \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n238: ]\n239: \n240: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n241:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\":     {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"implement\", \"build\", \"create\", \"feature\", \"component\", \"module\", \"authoring\"]},\n242:     \"CODE_QUALITY\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"lint\", \"format\", \"quality gate\", \"naming\", \"standards\", \"code smell\", \"comment hygiene\", \"restraint\", \"yagni\", \"simplify\", \"over-engineering\", \"bloat\", \"skipped_untrusted\", \"untrusted repository check\", \"trustrepochecks\", \"quality config\", \"repository/quality.json\", \"configured check\", \"unverified\"]},\n243:     \"DEBUGGING\":          {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"debug\", \"broken\", \"failing\", \"stack trace\", \"console error\", \"regression\"]},\n244:     \"VERIFICATION\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"verify\", \"passing\", \"type-check\", \"alignment drift\", \"completion claim\"]},\n245:     \"HOOKS\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"session-prime\", \"user-prompt-submit\", \"pre-tool-use\", \"post-tool-use\"]},\n246:     \"CONFIG\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"jsonc\", \".json\", \".jsonc\", \"descriptor\", \"config schema\"]},\n247:     \"JAVASCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"javascript\", \"vanilla js\", \".js\", \"commonjs\", \".cjs\", \".mjs\"]},\n248:     \"TYPESCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"typescript\", \".ts\", \".tsx\"]},\n249:     \"PYTHON\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"python\", \".py\", \"docstring\"]},\n250:     \"SHELL\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"shell script\", \"bash\", \".sh\"]},\n251:     \"RUST\":               {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"rust\", \".rs\", \"cargo.toml\", \"cargo.lock\", \"napi-rs\", \"napi_rs\", \"#[napi]\", \"wasm-bindgen\", \"wasm_bindgen\", \"#[wasm_bindgen]\", \"wasi\", \"cdylib\"]},\n252:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"github actions workflow\", \"ci workflow\", \"github actions yaml\", \"workflow path filters\", \"github workflow timeout\", \"github workflow concurrency\", \"github workflow permissions\", \"required status check workflow\", \"ci job\", \"action to a sha\", \"sha-pinned action\", \"github actions permissions\", \"ci workflows\", \"ci switch\", \"turn off ci\", \".github/workflows\", \"workflow concurrency\", \"github actions release workflow\", \"github actions deploy workflow\", \"release workflow yaml\", \"deploy workflow yaml\", \"github actions minutes\", \"actions minutes\", \"actions runner minutes\", \"runner minutes\", \"ci workflow cost\", \"ci cost\", \"reduce ci costs\", \"lower ci costs\", \"cut ci costs\", \"reduce our ci costs\", \"lower our ci costs\", \"ci spend\", \"ci bill\", \"ci minutes cost\", \"cut the ci minutes\", \"cut ci minutes\", \"reduce ci minutes\", \"ci pipeline cost\", \"ci pipeline cheaper\", \"ci pipelines cheaper\", \"cheaper ci pipeline\", \"ci runs cheaper\", \"failing ci\", \"failing ci pipeline\", \"ci failure\", \"ci failures\", \"ci is failing\", \"broken ci\", \"debug the ci\", \"debug ci\", \"cost of our ci\", \"make ci cheaper\", \"github actions bill\", \"github actions cost\", \"turn off the ci\", \"turn on the ci\", \"ci review workflow\"]},\n253:     \"FRONTEND_WEB\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"semantic html markup\"]},\n254:     \"WEB_ELEMENTS\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"html form autocomplete\", \"dialog element\", \"popover attribute\"]},\n255:     \"WEB_CASCADE\":         {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"css cascade layers\", \"css specificity conflict\"]},\n256:     \"WEB_LAYOUT\":          {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"container query layout\", \"css stacking context\"]},\n257:     \"WEB_A11Y\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"web accessibility pattern\", \"focus visible styling\", \"keyboard focus\", \"broken tab order\", \"accessible name\", \"aria live region\", \"focus ring accessibility\", \"focus outline removed\", \"screen reader support\", \"aria label missing\", \"alt text missing\", \"wcag audit\"]},\n258:     \"WEB_PERF\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"web font loading\", \"largest contentful paint\"]},\n259:     \"WEB_I18N\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"right to left layout\", \"bidirectional text\"]},\n260:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"what padding should this have\", \"how much padding\", \"what font size should this be\", \"spacing between elements\", \"spacing scale\", \"type scale\", \"build a color palette\", \"shade ramp\", \"box shadow elevation\", \"border radius consistency\", \"contrast ratio failure\", \"grey text on colored background\", \"dark mode palette\", \"concentric border radius\", \"layered shadows\", \"shadow color on dark surface\", \"tabular numbers\", \"what should this button look like\", \"colour decision\", \"color decision\", \"padding\", \"type scale for\", \"font size\", \"colour palette\", \"color palette\", \"contrast ratio\", \"corner radius\", \"box shadow\", \"spacing between\", \"contrast of\", \"check the contrast\", \"colour contrast\", \"color contrast\", \"muted text\"]},\n261:     \"WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"ui looks off\", \"looks amateur\", \"feels cluttered\", \"make this look better\", \"make this ui look designed\", \"visual hierarchy\", \"no visual hierarchy\", \"nothing draws the eye\", \"everything competes for attention\", \"everything looks the same\", \"emphasize by de-emphasizing\", \"how to style a destructive button\", \"this page feels overwhelming\", \"where do i start designing\", \"design a new screen from scratch\", \"how much white space\", \"why does this look wrong\", \"does this look right\", \"critique this design\", \"design critique\", \"visual audit\", \"design review findings\", \"design review\", \"review this design\", \"review this screen\", \"ux laws\", \"cognitive load\", \"progressive disclosure\", \"empty state design\", \"look wrong\", \"looks wrong\", \"looks off\", \"visual review\", \"type hierarchy\"]},\n262:     \"WEB_MOTION\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"animation duration\", \"transition duration\", \"easing curve\", \"spring versus easing\", \"twelve principles of animation\", \"when not to animate\", \"response time budget\", \"touch target size\", \"hover state\", \"active pressed state\", \"disabled state styling\", \"button states\", \"animation\", \"transition timing\", \"easing\", \"hover\", \"focus-visible\", \"interaction states\"]},\n263:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\":      {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"figma to code\", \"check built screen against figma\", \"compare build with figma frame\", \"figma frame\", \"figma frame check\", \"implement a figma design\"]},\n264: }\n265: \n266: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n267:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\": [\n268:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\n269:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md\",\n270:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md\",\n271:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md\",\n272:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md\",\n273:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md\",\n274:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md\",\n275:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md\",\n276:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md\",\n277: \n278:     ],\n279:     \"CODE_QUALITY\": [\n280:         \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n281:         \"shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md\",\n282:         \"sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md\",\n283:         \"sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md\",\n284:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md\",  # SKILLED only: the house checklist loads when SKILLED resolves, never for a foreign surface\n285:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\",\n286:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md\",\n287:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md\",\n288:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md\",\n289:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md\",\n290:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md\",\n291:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md\",\n292:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md\",\n293:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md\",\n294:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md\",\n295:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md\",\n296:         \"sk-code-review/assets/code-quality-checklist.md\",\n297: \n298:     ],\n299:     \"DEBUGGING\": [\n300:         \"shared/references/workflow-debug.md\",\n301: \n302:     ],\n303:     \"VERIFICATION\": [\n304:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md\",\n305:         \"shared/references/workflow-verify.md\",\n306:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/README.md\",\n307: \n308:     ],\n309:     \"HOOKS\": [\n310:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/hooks.md\",\n311:     ],\n312:     \"CONFIG\": [\n313:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/style-guide.md\",\n314:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/quality-standards.md\",\n315:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/quick-reference.md\",\n316:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md\",\n317:     ],\n318:     \"JAVASCRIPT\": [\n319:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md\",\n320:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md\",\n321:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md\",\n322:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quick-reference.md\",\n323:     ],\n324:     \"TYPESCRIPT\": [\n325:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md\",\n326:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md\",\n327:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md\",\n328:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md\",\n329:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md\",\n330:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md\",\n331:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md\",\n332:     ],\n333:     \"PYTHON\": [\n334:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md\",\n335:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md\",\n336:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/quick-reference.md\",\n337:     ],\n338:     \"SHELL\": [\n339:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md\",\n340:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md\",\n341:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md\",\n342:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md\",\n343:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md\",\n344:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md\",\n345:     ],\n346:     \"RUST\": [\n347:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md\",\n348:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md\",\n349:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\",\n350:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md\",\n351:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md\",\n352:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md\",\n353:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md\",\n354:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md\",\n355:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md\",\n356:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md\",\n357:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md\",\n358:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md\",\n359:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md\",\n360:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md\",\n361:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md\",\n362:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md\",\n363:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md\",\n364:     ],\n365:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\": [\n366:         \"sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md\",\n367:         \"sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md\",\n368:         \"sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md\",\n369:         \"sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md\",\n370:     ],\n371:     \"FRONTEND_WEB\": [\n372:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n373:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n374:     ],\n375:     \"WEB_ELEMENTS\": [\n376:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n377:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n378:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-elements-and-attributes.md\",\n379:     ],\n380:     \"WEB_CASCADE\": [\n381:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n382:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n383:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md\",\n384:     ],\n385:     \"WEB_LAYOUT\": [\n386:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n387:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n388:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-layout-and-sizing.md\",\n389:     ],\n390:     \"WEB_A11Y\": [\n391:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n392:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n393:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md\",\n394:     ],\n395:     \"WEB_PERF\": [\n396:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n397:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n398:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-performance-and-loading.md\",\n399:     ],\n400:     \"WEB_I18N\": [\n401:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n402:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n403:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-internationalization.md\",\n404:     ],\n405:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": [\n406:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n407:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md\",\n408:     ],\n409:     \"WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY\": [\n410:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n411:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md\",\n412:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md\",\n413:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md\",\n414:     ],\n415:     \"WEB_MOTION\": [\n416:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n417:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-motion-and-interaction.md\",\n418:     ],\n419:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\": [\n420:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md\",\n421:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n422:     ],\n423: }\n424: \n425: # Hub-level shared controls, declared once here. This list holds every\n426: # contained shared/ path that RESOURCE_MAP references but that is exempt from\n427: # typed-leaf projection, because it has no single packet owner. The root-router\n428: # contract validates each entry and requires RESOURCE_MAP to reference it.\n429: # DEFAULT_RESOURCE above is the other half of the set: its always-loaded\n430: # preamble paths are hub-level controls too. No other shared/ path is a\n431: # control, so a guard that needs the control set reads these two lists.\n432: SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES = [\n433:     \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n434:     \"shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md\",\n435:     \"shared/references/workflow-debug.md\",\n436:     \"shared/references/workflow-verify.md\",\n437: ]\n438: ```\n439: \n440: ### Surface-aware loading (route-time)\n441: \n442: The router does NOT load the whole matched-intent union. After surface detection (§1, `stack-detection.md`), a route loads:\n443: \n444: - the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble (stack/phase detection and the universal quality baseline), plus\n445: - the `shared/` entries the matched intents map (for example `code-quality-standards.md` under CODE_QUALITY, `workflow-debug.md` under DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` under VERIFICATION), never the whole `shared/references/universal/` folder, plus\n446: - only the **detected surface's** slice (`<surface>/references/*`) for the matched intents.\n447: \n448: - It does not load another surface's resources.\n449: - It defers `assets/*` (checklists, recipes, templates) to on-demand rather than the first slice.\n450: - Within SKILLED it slices once more by the **detected language** (§1 sub-detection): a TypeScript task loads `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/*` plus the language-agnostic `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`, not the Python, shell, config, or JavaScript folders.\n451: - An `UNKNOWN` surface falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map.\n452: \n453: This is what stops a routine single-surface task from pulling the full cross-surface set. The deterministic router-replay enforces the same rule, so the benchmark measures it.\n454: \n455: ---\n456: \n457: ## 9. HOW TO READ THIS\n458: \n459: - Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n460: - One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n461: - A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n462: - No keyword match, or an `UNKNOWN` surface, falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map — confirm the surface and intent before loading more. See §6 (UNKNOWN FALLBACK) and §7 (LOADING DISCIPLINE) for the full rules.\n463: \n464: ---\n465: \n466: ## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n467: \n468: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) + SKILLED language sub-detection\n469: - [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and per-phase resource loading\n470: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n471: - Barter equivalent: `barter/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/smart-routing.md` (different routing key — git-remote project — but same structural pattern)\n472: \n473: ---\n474: \n475: ## 11. MACHINE ROUTER\n476: \n477: The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 8 stays the only replay source.\n478: \n479: > **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n480: > ```bash\n481: > node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"\n482: > ```\n483: > Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n484: \n485: **Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\n486: Then add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\n487: When the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\n488: Workflow modes keep first place, and the targets and the bundle kind stay the same.\n489: `SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\n490: With `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n491: \n492: ### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n493: \n494: Root `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\n495: The hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §8: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n496: The hub router keeps stage-one mode selection and its `shared/README.md` fallback.\n497: \n498: ### The discriminator\n499: - **`workflowMode`** - the public mode/packet key: `sk-code-quality`, `sk-code-review` (workflow) or `sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend`, `sk-code-skilled` (surface).\n500: - **`packetKind`** - the axis: `workflow` (a mode that acts) or `surface` (read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode).\n501: - **`backendKind`** - which backend runs the packet: `surface-router` or `review-cache` for workflow modes, `evidence-base` for surface packets.\n502: \n503: Surface packets are advisor-invisible (`routingClass: metadata`, read-only `toolSurface`).\n504: The advisor still routes the single identity `sk-code`, and the hub bundles zero-or-more surfaces as evidence via `routerPolicy.outcomes.surfaceBundle`.\n505: The workflow mode comes first, followed by surfaces.\n506: \"review my typescript module for jank\" → `[sk-code-review, sk-code-skilled]`.\n507: Bundled surfaces follow the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order. It ranks `sk-code-frontend` ahead of `sk-code-ci`, then `sk-code-skilled` last, because a new surface goes ahead of the existing ones and the SKILLED fallback stays last.\n508: \n509: ### Surface detection\n510: \n511: Surface detection lives once in the hub's `shared/` layer and every mode and surface consumes it.\n512: It tries every declared specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n513: When a specific surface and `SKILLED` both match, the specific one leads and `SKILLED` comes along as second evidence.\n514: Each surface declares its markers in a `surfaceDetection` block in `mode-registry.json`, or in its own `graph-metadata.json` when it lives outside the hub.\n515: Modes own workflow contracts, not surface identity.\n516: `sk-code-ci` and `sk-code-frontend` are the surfaces that stack detection does not select. The hub bundles both from request vocabulary alone: CI through `code-ci-aliases` and `code-ci-runtime`, and Frontend through `code-frontend-aliases` in `hub-router.json`.\n517: \n518: ### Fallback route\n519: \n520: When no workflow mode dominates (a bare implement/debug/verify request), the router defers to a pure surface bundle: it detects the surface, loads that surface's evidence and workflow doctrine, and the agent acts. `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`, so the hub does not force a stale default. An unclear code intent asks for disambiguation.\n521: \n522: Per-mode behavior is **not flattened**: each packet keeps its own code-work contract, standards, evidence rules, and tool-permission guards.\n523: \n524: ### Routing rule\n525: ```python\n526: from pathlib import Path\n527: \n528: SKILL_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n529: UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n530:     \"Confirm whether this is sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend or sk-code-skilled work\",\n531:     \"Confirm the target files or runtime surface\",\n532:     \"Confirm the expected action: implement, debug, verify, quality gate, or review\",\n533:     \"Confirm the verification command set before completion\",\n534: ]\n535: \n536: def _guard_in_skill(relative_path: str) -> str:\n537:     resolved = (SKILL_ROOT / relative_path).resolve()\n538:     resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT)\n539:     if resolved.name != \"SKILL.md\" and resolved.suffix.lower() != \".md\":\n540:         raise ValueError(f\"Only markdown skill resources are routable: {relative_path}\")\n541:     return resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT).as_posix()\n542: \n543: def load_if_available(relative_path: str, loaded: list[str], seen: set[str]) -> None:\n544:     guarded = _guard_in_skill(relative_path)\n545:     path = SKILL_ROOT / guarded\n546:     if path.exists() and guarded not in seen:\n547:         load(guarded)\n548:         loaded.append(guarded)\n549:         seen.add(guarded)\n550: \n551: def route(task):\n552:     loaded, seen = [], set()\n553:     registry = read_json(SKILL_ROOT / \"mode-registry.json\")\n554:     workflow_mode = explicit_mode_hint(task) or classify_workflow_mode(task)\n555: \n556:     if not workflow_mode:\n557:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n558:         return {\n559:             \"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\",\n560:             \"needs_disambiguation\": True,\n561:             \"disambiguation_checklist\": UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST,\n562:             \"resources\": loaded,\n563:         }\n564: \n565:     entry = find_registry_entry(registry, workflowMode=workflow_mode)\n566:     if not entry:\n567:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n568:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"unknown_mode\": workflow_mode, \"resources\": loaded}\n569: \n570:     packet_skill = f\"{entry['packet']}/SKILL.md\"\n571:     load_if_available(packet_skill, loaded, seen)\n572: \n573:     for surface_mode in surface_bundle_from_hub_router(task):\n574:         surface_entry = find_registry_entry(registry, workflowMode=surface_mode)\n575:         if surface_entry:\n576:             load_if_available(f\"{surface_entry['packet']}/SKILL.md\", loaded, seen)\n577: \n578:     if not loaded:\n579:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n580:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"notice\": \"matched registry but no packet SKILL.md was available\", \"resources\": loaded}\n581: \n582:     return {\"workflowMode\": workflow_mode, \"backendKind\": entry[\"backendKind\"], \"resources\": loaded}\n583: ```","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md, lines 1-583\n1: ---\n2: title: sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n3: description: First-class surface router document at the sk-code hub root (ROUTER.md). hub-router.json selects the workflow mode; this doc maps a request's code intent to the exact packet-local leaf resources that mode should load, emitting canonical (workflowMode, leafResourceId) pairs. Relocated from shared/references/smart-routing.md (v3.5.0.8) as the ROUTER.md consolidation pattern, with the approved sk-code shared-control delta (router self-reference removed, ten hub-shared paths normalized to shared/..., mapped shared controls declared).\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code smart routing\"\n6:   - \"surface resource maps\"\n7:   - \"intent classification routing\"\n8:   - \"load tier resource loading\"\n9:   - \"unknown surface fallback\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 2.3.19.0\n13: router_state: active\n14: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n15: ---\n16: # sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n17: \n18: Leaf-set authority for sk-code. The routing authority is named once in `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING: `hub-router.json` is the mode vocabulary, and this document is the leaf-set router. Consolidates intent classification + resource loading into the single Barter-style smart-routing entry point. Detection logic lives in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md); phase lifecycle lives in [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: ### Purpose\n25: \n26: The smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n27: \n28: ### When to Use\n29: \n30: - After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n31: - When classifying task wording into the dominant work intent\n32: - When mapping intent to concrete reference paths\n33: - When picking verification commands for the surface\n34: \n35: ### Core Principle\n36: \n37: Routing is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n38: \n39: ### Key Sources\n40: \n41: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n42: - [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and phase-specific resource expectations\n43: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n48: \n49: Intent classification scores task text against weighted keyword signals to pick the dominant work type after surface detection. The vocabulary and weights are authored once, in the `INTENT_SIGNALS` block of §8 MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER; read them there. This section is the human-facing summary of how that block is scored, never a second copy of its keywords.\n50: \n51: ### Scoring algorithm\n52: \n53: 1. Sum weighted keyword hits from the request, target files, and known task context.\n54: 2. Boost explicit phase signals (verification, debugging, code quality) by +5 when the phase is unambiguous.\n55: 3. Select the top intent.\n56: 4. Select a second intent when the score delta to the next intent is small (`AMBIGUITY_DELTA = 1`).\n57: 5. For SKILLED surface, run language sub-detection after intent selection.\n58: \n59: ### Doc-only edit anti-signals\n60: \n61: Doc-only prose changes subtract from `sk-code` intent scoring and add to `sk-doc` scoring, even when the target file is under `.skilled/skills/`.\n62: \n63: | Prompt signal | Effect |\n64: | --- | --- |\n65: | `update headline`, `rewrite headline`, `headline section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n66: | `clarify description`, `clarify the X model`, `documentation section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n67: | `add a one-line summary`, `add summary at the top` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n68: | `improve readme`, `reorganize readme`, `rewrite section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n69: | `SKILL.md` or `README.md` plus prose-only verbs | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n70: \n71: Do not apply this anti-signal when the same request asks to modify executable code, parser logic, tests, shell behavior, TypeScript/Python entrypoints, JSON schema behavior, or routing algorithms. In that case, keep SKILLED surface detection and load `sk-code`.\n72: \n73: Multi-symptom prompts like `debug the broken jsonc descriptor` should load both DEBUGGING and CONFIG. Prompts like `update TypeScript advisor fixture` should load SKILLED plus TYPESCRIPT and CODE_QUALITY.\n74: \n75: ### Surface-specific intent notes\n76: \n77: - **SKILLED** intent scoring favors language standards, hooks, config, scripts, advisor/tests, metadata, and alignment verification.\n78: - Unsupported surfaces stay UNKNOWN even if intent scoring is strong.\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 3. LOAD TIERS\n83: \n84: | Tier | When | Resources |\n85: | --- | --- | --- |\n86: | ALWAYS | Every invocation | The §8 `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble only: `shared/references/stack-detection.md`, `shared/references/phase-detection.md` and `shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`. The style guide `sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md` loads with CODE_QUALITY, and the shared doctrine loads with its intent: `workflow-debug.md` with DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` with VERIFICATION |\n87: | SURFACE | After SKILLED detection | Surface-specific resources (`sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`) |\n88: | INTENT | After intent classification | Implementation, debugging, verification, testing, etc. matching the top-1 intent (and top-2 when ambiguous) |\n89: | LANGUAGE | SKILLED only | Split JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and Config standards from the matching `sk-code-skilled/references/<lang>/` resources |\n90: | ON_DEMAND | Explicit deep-dive keywords | Extended checklists and niche references (e.g. `sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md` for boundary templates) |\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 4. SKILLED MAP\n95: \n96: SKILLED loads from `sk-code-skilled/references/` and `sk-code-skilled/assets/`.\n97: \n98: ### Implementation shared tier\n99: \n100: IMPLEMENTATION loads the shared organization and naming baseline:\n101: \n102: - `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`\n103: - `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`\n104: \n105: ### Intent overlay\n106: \n107: | Intent | Resources |\n108: | --- | --- |\n109: | CODE_QUALITY | SKILLED only: `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` plus the language-matching checklist, both when SKILLED is a resolved surface. A foreign surface loads the floor in `code-quality-standards.md` and its own declared rows |\n110: | VERIFICATION | `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` |\n111: | HOOKS | `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/hooks.md` |\n112: | CONFIG | `sk-code-skilled/references/config/*` |\n113: | LANGUAGE_STANDARDS | Detected language folder's `quick-reference.md`, `style-guide.md`, `quality-standards.md` |\n114: \n115: ### Language overlay\n116: \n117: | Language | Resources |\n118: | --- | --- |\n119: | JAVASCRIPT | `sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n120: | TYPESCRIPT | `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n121: | PYTHON | `sk-code-skilled/references/python/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n122: | SHELL | `sk-code-skilled/references/shell/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n123: | RUST | `sk-code-skilled/references/rust/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}/` (each split into topic parts), `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/` |\n124: | CONFIG | `sk-code-skilled/references/config/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n125: \n126: ### Authoring checklist gating\n127: \n128: When SKILLED intent is `authoring-new-X`, additionally load the matching authoring checklist:\n129: \n130: | Authoring target | Checklist |\n131: | --- | --- |\n132: | New skill | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n133: | New agent | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n134: | New command | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n135: | New MCP server | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md` |\n136: | Spec folder write | Now in system-spec-kit: `system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` + `system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` |\n137: \n138: ### CI Workflow Map\n139: \n140: When the request names a GitHub Actions workflow, the hub bundles `sk-code-ci` from the request vocabulary and the `CI_WORKFLOW` intent loads its four rule references:\n141: \n142: | Resource | Carries |\n143: | --- | --- |\n144: | `sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` | The rule index: path parity, SHA pins, README row, `set -euo pipefail`, fork and secret safety, matrix and `fail-fast` |\n145: | `sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md` | The twin rule, the rebuild-commit skip rule, push-only scope and the intentional exceptions table |\n146: | `sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md` | Timeouts, permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch `if:` shape |\n147: | `sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md` | The repository CI profile and its keys, the workflows protected by function and the rules that follow |\n148: \n149: The map is the packet's own `RESOURCE_MAP` re-prefixed with `sk-code-ci/`, so the two stay equal.\n150: \n151: ### Frontend Map\n152: \n153: When a request asks about web frontend practice, or a Figma-to-code check, the hub bundles `sk-code-frontend` from request vocabulary alone:\n154: \n155: | Resource | Carries |\n156: | --- | --- |\n157: | `sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md` | The cross-stack core, token precedence, Figma boundary, and Frontend overrides contract |\n158: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md` | The web stack layer for markup, forms, layout, responsive behavior, media, and CSS architecture |\n159: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-elements-and-attributes.md` | Form autocomplete, dialog, and popover behavior |\n160: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md` | Cascade layers and specificity conflicts |\n161: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-layout-and-sizing.md` | Container queries and stacking contexts |\n162: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md` | Accessibility patterns, visible focus, and live regions |\n163: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-performance-and-loading.md` | Web font loading and largest contentful paint |\n164: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-internationalization.md` | Right-to-left layout and bidirectional text |\n165: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md` | Visual value decisions: spacing, type, weight, colour, shadow, radius, opacity, palettes and token sets |\n166: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md` | Visual hierarchy and review: build procedure, symptom-to-fix tables, UX laws and the review pass |\n167: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-motion-and-interaction.md` | Motion and interaction: animation, input, touch, focus, performance and feedback rules |\n168: | `sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md` | The five-pass check for comparing a built screen with its Figma frame |\n169: \n170: The map is the packet's own `RESOURCE_MAP` for the web, visual and Figma-to-code intents, re-prefixed with `sk-code-frontend/`, so the two stay equal there.\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## 5. VERIFICATION COMMANDS\n175: \n176: | Surface | Required verification evidence |\n177: | --- | --- |\n178: | SKILLED | `python3 .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py --root <changed-scope>`; plus targeted language/project tests such as vitest, pytest, shellcheck, JSON validation, or spec validation for changed spec folders |\n179: | UNKNOWN | User-selected verification command set before completion claim |\n180: \n181: ---\n182: \n183: ## 6. UNKNOWN FALLBACK\n184: \n185: If no supported surface matches, ask:\n186: \n187: 1. Is this `.skilled/` system code, or an unsupported stack?\n188: 2. Which files or directories are changing?\n189: 3. Which verification command proves the claim?\n190: 4. Should a new `sk-code` route be planned before implementation?\n191: \n192: Canonical sk-code owns the SKILLED surface, the `sk-code-ci` GitHub Actions surface and the stack-agnostic `sk-code-frontend` surface for web practice. Go, Next.js server code and React Native surfaces live in a project fork.\n193: \n194: ### UNKNOWN fallback checklist\n195: \n196: Returned when intent confidence is low (`max(intent_scores) < 0.5`) OR when the user explicitly asks for stack-agnostic guidance:\n197: \n198: - Confirm the detected surface (a declared specific surface, the SKILLED fallback or UNKNOWN)\n199: - Confirm the task intent (implementation / debugging / verification / etc.)\n200: - Provide one concrete input, error, or expected output\n201: - Confirm the verification command set before completion\n202: \n203: ---\n204: \n205: ## 7. LOADING DISCIPLINE (DO / DON'T)\n206: \n207: ### DO\n208: \n209: - Load only references needed for current intent + surface\n210: - Use exact canonical file paths in playbook result YAML (never directory placeholders)\n211: - Cite surface + top-1 intent + loaded resource paths + verification command in your final answer\n212: \n213: ### DON'T\n214: \n215: - Load all of `sk-code-skilled/` indiscriminately (\"just in case\")\n216: - Load Go, Next.js server code or React Native resources from canonical sk-code; those live in a project fork\n217: - Drop directory placeholders in playbook result YAML\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 8. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n222: \n223: This block is the authored source for the intent vocabulary and weights. §2 explains how it is scored and does not repeat the keywords; §4 renders the same rows as prose. When an intent row changes here, update the matching prose row in §4.\n224: \n225: This projection is intentionally lossy in two documented ways the flat dictionary cannot express, both enforced by the prose contract and the surface-detection pseudocode in this file's §11:\n226: \n227: - **No phase boosts.** The `+5` unambiguous-phase boost from the §2 scoring algorithm is not represented; every keyword carries unit weight here.\n228: - **No doc-only anti-signals.** The `sk-code -2 / sk-doc +3` anti-signals (§2) that route prose-only edits away from this skill are a pre-filter the flat model does not carry.\n229: \n230: ```python\n231: # Always-loaded routing preamble: every code task detects stack, detects phase,\n232: # consults the router, and applies the universal quality baseline before any\n233: # surface-specific resource. These are loaded on every route.\n234: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n235:     \"shared/references/stack-detection.md\",\n236:     \"shared/references/phase-detection.md\",\n237:     \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n238: ]\n239: \n240: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n241:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\":     {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"implement\", \"build\", \"create\", \"feature\", \"component\", \"module\", \"authoring\"]},\n242:     \"CODE_QUALITY\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"lint\", \"format\", \"quality gate\", \"naming\", \"standards\", \"code smell\", \"comment hygiene\", \"restraint\", \"yagni\", \"simplify\", \"over-engineering\", \"bloat\", \"skipped_untrusted\", \"untrusted repository check\", \"trustrepochecks\", \"quality config\", \"repository/quality.json\", \"configured check\", \"unverified\"]},\n243:     \"DEBUGGING\":          {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"debug\", \"broken\", \"failing\", \"stack trace\", \"console error\", \"regression\"]},\n244:     \"VERIFICATION\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"verify\", \"passing\", \"type-check\", \"alignment drift\", \"completion claim\"]},\n245:     \"HOOKS\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"session-prime\", \"user-prompt-submit\", \"pre-tool-use\", \"post-tool-use\"]},\n246:     \"CONFIG\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"jsonc\", \".json\", \".jsonc\", \"descriptor\", \"config schema\"]},\n247:     \"JAVASCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"javascript\", \"vanilla js\", \".js\", \"commonjs\", \".cjs\", \".mjs\"]},\n248:     \"TYPESCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"typescript\", \".ts\", \".tsx\"]},\n249:     \"PYTHON\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"python\", \".py\", \"docstring\"]},\n250:     \"SHELL\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"shell script\", \"bash\", \".sh\"]},\n251:     \"RUST\":               {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"rust\", \".rs\", \"cargo.toml\", \"cargo.lock\", \"napi-rs\", \"napi_rs\", \"#[napi]\", \"wasm-bindgen\", \"wasm_bindgen\", \"#[wasm_bindgen]\", \"wasi\", \"cdylib\"]},\n252:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"github actions workflow\", \"ci workflow\", \"github actions yaml\", \"workflow path filters\", \"github workflow timeout\", \"github workflow concurrency\", \"github workflow permissions\", \"required status check workflow\", \"ci job\", \"action to a sha\", \"sha-pinned action\", \"github actions permissions\", \"ci workflows\", \"ci switch\", \"turn off ci\", \".github/workflows\", \"workflow concurrency\", \"github actions release workflow\", \"github actions deploy workflow\", \"release workflow yaml\", \"deploy workflow yaml\", \"github actions minutes\", \"actions minutes\", \"actions runner minutes\", \"runner minutes\", \"ci workflow cost\", \"ci cost\", \"reduce ci costs\", \"lower ci costs\", \"cut ci costs\", \"reduce our ci costs\", \"lower our ci costs\", \"ci spend\", \"ci bill\", \"ci minutes cost\", \"cut the ci minutes\", \"cut ci minutes\", \"reduce ci minutes\", \"ci pipeline cost\", \"ci pipeline cheaper\", \"ci pipelines cheaper\", \"cheaper ci pipeline\", \"ci runs cheaper\", \"failing ci\", \"failing ci pipeline\", \"ci failure\", \"ci failures\", \"ci is failing\", \"broken ci\", \"debug the ci\", \"debug ci\", \"cost of our ci\", \"make ci cheaper\", \"github actions bill\", \"github actions cost\", \"turn off the ci\", \"turn on the ci\", \"ci review workflow\"]},\n253:     \"FRONTEND_WEB\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"semantic html markup\"]},\n254:     \"WEB_ELEMENTS\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"html form autocomplete\", \"dialog element\", \"popover attribute\"]},\n255:     \"WEB_CASCADE\":         {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"css cascade layers\", \"css specificity conflict\"]},\n256:     \"WEB_LAYOUT\":          {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"container query layout\", \"css stacking context\"]},\n257:     \"WEB_A11Y\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"web accessibility pattern\", \"focus visible styling\", \"keyboard focus\", \"broken tab order\", \"accessible name\", \"aria live region\", \"focus ring accessibility\", \"focus outline removed\", \"screen reader support\", \"aria label missing\", \"alt text missing\", \"wcag audit\"]},\n258:     \"WEB_PERF\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"web font loading\", \"largest contentful paint\"]},\n259:     \"WEB_I18N\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"right to left layout\", \"bidirectional text\"]},\n260:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"what padding should this have\", \"how much padding\", \"what font size should this be\", \"spacing between elements\", \"spacing scale\", \"type scale\", \"build a color palette\", \"shade ramp\", \"box shadow elevation\", \"border radius consistency\", \"contrast ratio failure\", \"grey text on colored background\", \"dark mode palette\", \"concentric border radius\", \"layered shadows\", \"shadow color on dark surface\", \"tabular numbers\", \"what should this button look like\", \"colour decision\", \"color decision\", \"padding\", \"type scale for\", \"font size\", \"colour palette\", \"color palette\", \"contrast ratio\", \"corner radius\", \"box shadow\", \"spacing between\", \"contrast of\", \"check the contrast\", \"colour contrast\", \"color contrast\", \"muted text\"]},\n261:     \"WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"ui looks off\", \"looks amateur\", \"feels cluttered\", \"make this look better\", \"make this ui look designed\", \"visual hierarchy\", \"no visual hierarchy\", \"nothing draws the eye\", \"everything competes for attention\", \"everything looks the same\", \"emphasize by de-emphasizing\", \"how to style a destructive button\", \"this page feels overwhelming\", \"where do i start designing\", \"design a new screen from scratch\", \"how much white space\", \"why does this look wrong\", \"does this look right\", \"critique this design\", \"design critique\", \"visual audit\", \"design review findings\", \"design review\", \"review this design\", \"review this screen\", \"ux laws\", \"cognitive load\", \"progressive disclosure\", \"empty state design\", \"look wrong\", \"looks wrong\", \"looks off\", \"visual review\", \"type hierarchy\"]},\n262:     \"WEB_MOTION\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"animation duration\", \"transition duration\", \"easing curve\", \"spring versus easing\", \"twelve principles of animation\", \"when not to animate\", \"response time budget\", \"touch target size\", \"hover state\", \"active pressed state\", \"disabled state styling\", \"button states\", \"animation\", \"transition timing\", \"easing\", \"hover\", \"focus-visible\", \"interaction states\"]},\n263:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\":      {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"figma to code\", \"check built screen against figma\", \"compare build with figma frame\", \"figma frame\", \"figma frame check\", \"implement a figma design\"]},\n264: }\n265: \n266: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n267:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\": [\n268:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\n269:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md\",\n270:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md\",\n271:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md\",\n272:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md\",\n273:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md\",\n274:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md\",\n275:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md\",\n276:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md\",\n277: \n278:     ],\n279:     \"CODE_QUALITY\": [\n280:         \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n281:         \"shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md\",\n282:         \"sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md\",\n283:         \"sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md\",\n284:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md\",  # SKILLED only: the house checklist loads when SKILLED resolves, never for a foreign surface\n285:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\",\n286:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md\",\n287:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md\",\n288:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md\",\n289:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md\",\n290:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md\",\n291:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md\",\n292:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md\",\n293:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md\",\n294:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md\",\n295:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md\",\n296:         \"sk-code-review/assets/code-quality-checklist.md\",\n297: \n298:     ],\n299:     \"DEBUGGING\": [\n300:         \"shared/references/workflow-debug.md\",\n301: \n302:     ],\n303:     \"VERIFICATION\": [\n304:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md\",\n305:         \"shared/references/workflow-verify.md\",\n306:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/README.md\",\n307: \n308:     ],\n309:     \"HOOKS\": [\n310:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/hooks.md\",\n311:     ],\n312:     \"CONFIG\": [\n313:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/style-guide.md\",\n314:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/quality-standards.md\",\n315:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/quick-reference.md\",\n316:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md\",\n317:     ],\n318:     \"JAVASCRIPT\": [\n319:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md\",\n320:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md\",\n321:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md\",\n322:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quick-reference.md\",\n323:     ],\n324:     \"TYPESCRIPT\": [\n325:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md\",\n326:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md\",\n327:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md\",\n328:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md\",\n329:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md\",\n330:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md\",\n331:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md\",\n332:     ],\n333:     \"PYTHON\": [\n334:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md\",\n335:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md\",\n336:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/quick-reference.md\",\n337:     ],\n338:     \"SHELL\": [\n339:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md\",\n340:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md\",\n341:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md\",\n342:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md\",\n343:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md\",\n344:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md\",\n345:     ],\n346:     \"RUST\": [\n347:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md\",\n348:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md\",\n349:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\",\n350:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md\",\n351:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md\",\n352:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md\",\n353:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md\",\n354:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md\",\n355:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md\",\n356:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md\",\n357:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md\",\n358:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md\",\n359:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md\",\n360:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md\",\n361:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md\",\n362:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md\",\n363:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md\",\n364:     ],\n365:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\": [\n366:         \"sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md\",\n367:         \"sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md\",\n368:         \"sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md\",\n369:         \"sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md\",\n370:     ],\n371:     \"FRONTEND_WEB\": [\n372:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n373:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n374:     ],\n375:     \"WEB_ELEMENTS\": [\n376:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n377:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n378:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-elements-and-attributes.md\",\n379:     ],\n380:     \"WEB_CASCADE\": [\n381:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n382:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n383:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md\",\n384:     ],\n385:     \"WEB_LAYOUT\": [\n386:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n387:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n388:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-layout-and-sizing.md\",\n389:     ],\n390:     \"WEB_A11Y\": [\n391:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n392:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n393:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md\",\n394:     ],\n395:     \"WEB_PERF\": [\n396:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n397:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n398:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-performance-and-loading.md\",\n399:     ],\n400:     \"WEB_I18N\": [\n401:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n402:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n403:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-internationalization.md\",\n404:     ],\n405:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": [\n406:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n407:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md\",\n408:     ],\n409:     \"WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY\": [\n410:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n411:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md\",\n412:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md\",\n413:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md\",\n414:     ],\n415:     \"WEB_MOTION\": [\n416:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n417:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-motion-and-interaction.md\",\n418:     ],\n419:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\": [\n420:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md\",\n421:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n422:     ],\n423: }\n424: \n425: # Hub-level shared controls, declared once here. This list holds every\n426: # contained shared/ path that RESOURCE_MAP references but that is exempt from\n427: # typed-leaf projection, because it has no single packet owner. The root-router\n428: # contract validates each entry and requires RESOURCE_MAP to reference it.\n429: # DEFAULT_RESOURCE above is the other half of the set: its always-loaded\n430: # preamble paths are hub-level controls too. No other shared/ path is a\n431: # control, so a guard that needs the control set reads these two lists.\n432: SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES = [\n433:     \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n434:     \"shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md\",\n435:     \"shared/references/workflow-debug.md\",\n436:     \"shared/references/workflow-verify.md\",\n437: ]\n438: ```\n439: \n440: ### Surface-aware loading (route-time)\n441: \n442: The router does NOT load the whole matched-intent union. After surface detection (§1, `stack-detection.md`), a route loads:\n443: \n444: - the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble (stack/phase detection and the universal quality baseline), plus\n445: - the `shared/` entries the matched intents map (for example `code-quality-standards.md` under CODE_QUALITY, `workflow-debug.md` under DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` under VERIFICATION), never the whole `shared/references/universal/` folder, plus\n446: - only the **detected surface's** slice (`<surface>/references/*`) for the matched intents.\n447: \n448: - It does not load another surface's resources.\n449: - It defers `assets/*` (checklists, recipes, templates) to on-demand rather than the first slice.\n450: - Within SKILLED it slices once more by the **detected language** (§1 sub-detection): a TypeScript task loads `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/*` plus the language-agnostic `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`, not the Python, shell, config, or JavaScript folders.\n451: - An `UNKNOWN` surface falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map.\n452: \n453: This is what stops a routine single-surface task from pulling the full cross-surface set. The deterministic router-replay enforces the same rule, so the benchmark measures it.\n454: \n455: ---\n456: \n457: ## 9. HOW TO READ THIS\n458: \n459: - Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n460: - One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n461: - A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n462: - No keyword match, or an `UNKNOWN` surface, falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map — confirm the surface and intent before loading more. See §6 (UNKNOWN FALLBACK) and §7 (LOADING DISCIPLINE) for the full rules.\n463: \n464: ---\n465: \n466: ## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n467: \n468: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) + SKILLED language sub-detection\n469: - [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and per-phase resource loading\n470: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n471: - Barter equivalent: `barter/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/smart-routing.md` (different routing key — git-remote project — but same structural pattern)\n472: \n473: ---\n474: \n475: ## 11. MACHINE ROUTER\n476: \n477: The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 8 stays the only replay source.\n478: \n479: > **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n480: > ```bash\n481: > node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"\n482: > ```\n483: > Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n484: \n485: **Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\n486: Then add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\n487: When the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\n488: Workflow modes keep first place, and the targets and the bundle kind stay the same.\n489: `SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\n490: With `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n491: \n492: ### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n493: \n494: Root `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\n495: The hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §8: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n496: The hub router keeps stage-one mode selection and its `shared/README.md` fallback.\n497: \n498: ### The discriminator\n499: - **`workflowMode`** - the public mode/packet key: `sk-code-quality`, `sk-code-review` (workflow) or `sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend`, `sk-code-skilled` (surface).\n500: - **`packetKind`** - the axis: `workflow` (a mode that acts) or `surface` (read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode).\n501: - **`backendKind`** - which backend runs the packet: `surface-router` or `review-cache` for workflow modes, `evidence-base` for surface packets.\n502: \n503: Surface packets are advisor-invisible (`routingClass: metadata`, read-only `toolSurface`).\n504: The advisor still routes the single identity `sk-code`, and the hub bundles zero-or-more surfaces as evidence via `routerPolicy.outcomes.surfaceBundle`.\n505: The workflow mode comes first, followed by surfaces.\n506: \"review my typescript module for jank\" → `[sk-code-review, sk-code-skilled]`.\n507: Bundled surfaces follow the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order. It ranks `sk-code-frontend` ahead of `sk-code-ci`, then `sk-code-skilled` last, because a new surface goes ahead of the existing ones and the SKILLED fallback stays last.\n508: \n509: ### Surface detection\n510: \n511: Surface detection lives once in the hub's `shared/` layer and every mode and surface consumes it.\n512: It tries every declared specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n513: When a specific surface and `SKILLED` both match, the specific one leads and `SKILLED` comes along as second evidence.\n514: Each surface declares its markers in a `surfaceDetection` block in `mode-registry.json`, or in its own `graph-metadata.json` when it lives outside the hub.\n515: Modes own workflow contracts, not surface identity.\n516: `sk-code-ci` and `sk-code-frontend` are the surfaces that stack detection does not select. The hub bundles both from request vocabulary alone: CI through `code-ci-aliases` and `code-ci-runtime`, and Frontend through `code-frontend-aliases` in `hub-router.json`.\n517: \n518: ### Fallback route\n519: \n520: When no workflow mode dominates (a bare implement/debug/verify request), the router defers to a pure surface bundle: it detects the surface, loads that surface's evidence and workflow doctrine, and the agent acts. `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`, so the hub does not force a stale default. An unclear code intent asks for disambiguation.\n521: \n522: Per-mode behavior is **not flattened**: each packet keeps its own code-work contract, standards, evidence rules, and tool-permission guards.\n523: \n524: ### Routing rule\n525: ```python\n526: from pathlib import Path\n527: \n528: SKILL_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n529: UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n530:     \"Confirm whether this is sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend or sk-code-skilled work\",\n531:     \"Confirm the target files or runtime surface\",\n532:     \"Confirm the expected action: implement, debug, verify, quality gate, or review\",\n533:     \"Confirm the verification command set before completion\",\n534: ]\n535: \n536: def _guard_in_skill(relative_path: str) -> str:\n537:     resolved = (SKILL_ROOT / relative_path).resolve()\n538:     resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT)\n539:     if resolved.name != \"SKILL.md\" and resolved.suffix.lower() != \".md\":\n540:         raise ValueError(f\"Only markdown skill resources are routable: {relative_path}\")\n541:     return resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT).as_posix()\n542: \n543: def load_if_available(relative_path: str, loaded: list[str], seen: set[str]) -> None:\n544:     guarded = _guard_in_skill(relative_path)\n545:     path = SKILL_ROOT / guarded\n546:     if path.exists() and guarded not in seen:\n547:         load(guarded)\n548:         loaded.append(guarded)\n549:         seen.add(guarded)\n550: \n551: def route(task):\n552:     loaded, seen = [], set()\n553:     registry = read_json(SKILL_ROOT / \"mode-registry.json\")\n554:     workflow_mode = explicit_mode_hint(task) or classify_workflow_mode(task)\n555: \n556:     if not workflow_mode:\n557:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n558:         return {\n559:             \"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\",\n560:             \"needs_disambiguation\": True,\n561:             \"disambiguation_checklist\": UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST,\n562:             \"resources\": loaded,\n563:         }\n564: \n565:     entry = find_registry_entry(registry, workflowMode=workflow_mode)\n566:     if not entry:\n567:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n568:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"unknown_mode\": workflow_mode, \"resources\": loaded}\n569: \n570:     packet_skill = f\"{entry['packet']}/SKILL.md\"\n571:     load_if_available(packet_skill, loaded, seen)\n572: \n573:     for surface_mode in surface_bundle_from_hub_router(task):\n574:         surface_entry = find_registry_entry(registry, workflowMode=surface_mode)\n575:         if surface_entry:\n576:             load_if_available(f\"{surface_entry['packet']}/SKILL.md\", loaded, seen)\n577: \n578:     if not loaded:\n579:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n580:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"notice\": \"matched registry but no packet SKILL.md was available\", \"resources\": loaded}\n581: \n582:     return {\"workflowMode\": workflow_mode, \"backendKind\": entry[\"backendKind\"], \"resources\": loaded}\n583: ```"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794535106895,"end":1794535107411}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794535112448,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"id":"prt_1d2a0bf00001iiweu5RPa5EBxf","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a08841001F3Dk0d13i56PXK","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"40c9cffa3bdc7376fd72fdb140056e48edce0483","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":930,"output":125,"reasoning":540,"cache":{"read":61824,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794535117141,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"id":"prt_1d2a0d155001xLSIHyfw0fwhih","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a0bf08001I0pDFyyLzyI0NF","type":"step-start","snapshot":"e5be96ecb422fe2e3cba34e6ee5dad8ce0451281"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794535130459,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"id":"prt_1d2a10056001FJdRtZrTmtU0sa","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a0bf08001I0pDFyyLzyI0NF","type":"text","text":"Route and convention confirmed: new doctor bridges append at `max(order)+1` in both lists (typescript 40, python 51). Loading the repo rules that fire for a config write and a reported result.","time":{"start":1794535129174,"end":1794535130459}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794535130473,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2a10487001tGlHRUeZmHV9wB","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a0bf08001I0pDFyyLzyI0NF","type":"tool","id":"call_94974079d59841caa2939421","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794535130247,"end":1794535130473}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794535130482,"sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2a102930022gWSBpNQJcbPV3","sessionID":"ses_e2d61a4abffeh9vQudGvgT5Z1T","messageID":"msg_1d2a0bf08001I0pDFyyLzyI0NF","type":"tool","id":"call_652b3389f8134debbebd62cd","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794535129747,"end":1794535130482}}}}
